 MRM transitions), sample processing (SPME), and internal standard (Lamivudine). The observed concentration in mouse control tumors (0.04 µM adenosine).\n*   **Auxiliary Material:** The biological context of inosine in the tumor microenvironment (TME), its effect on immune cells (T cells, macrophages), and the mechanism of adenosine-to-inosine conversion. This supports the *rationale* for quantifying these metabolites in the review, even if the specific protocol gap isn't filled.\n*   **Remaining Gap:** No human protocols, no human reference ranges, no SCFA protocols, no consensus guidelines.\n*   **Outline Action:** Use the method details as an example of preclinical quantification techniques. Note the lack of human standardization. Highlight the biological importance of inosine quantification as demonstrated here.\n\n**Constraints Checklist & Confidence Score:**\n1.  Explain direct contribution? Yes.\n2.  Preserve useful supporting material? Yes.\n3.  Keep primary gap judgment separate? Yes.\n4.  Use status (fulfilled, partial, unmet, unjudgeable)? Yes.\n5.  Use material_usefulness (direct, supporting, none)? Yes.\n6.  Do not call gap fulfilled while success criterion open? Yes (Criteria 1, 2, 3 all fail regarding *human* and *consensus*).\n7.  Describe what study studied/found first? Yes.\n8.  Explain how material helps gap? Yes.\n9.  Refer to paper as 'this study' or 'the authors'? Yes.\n10. Do not infer identity from reference list? Yes.\n11. Keep findings attached to study/research object? Yes.\n12. Do not rename method to fit gap? Yes (It's LC-MS/MS, but for mouse).\n13. Multiple data sources/transfer? Yes (Note mouse vs human).\n14. Do not infer design/validation from silence? Yes.\n15. Gap question describes planner wants, not facts? Yes.\n16. Never borrow requested stage to complete source description? Yes.\n17. Study-design label only when explicit? Yes.\n18. Remaining_gap/outline_action describe what material supports/unanswered? Yes.\n19. Review-reporte